==================================================
TASK 3: Explain Your Work in Your Own Words
==================================================
For this lab, I focused on building and organizing notebooks using just keyboard shortcuts like 'a', 'b', and 'm' without touching the mouse. I started with a default code cell, hit 'a' to insert a cell above, changed it to Markdown with 'm', and then used 'b' to add a code cell below. 

When I ran the cells, the output showed how Jupyter tracks execution using the `In [n]` counters. Even if a cell is near the top, if you execute it out of order, its number changes based on when it was run rather than where it sits on the screen. I also tested kernel state by defining a list, restarting the kernel, and running the print statement by itself. This immediately threw a `NameError` because restarting clears the kernel's active memory, proving that the visual layout and actual execution memory are completely separate.

The part that tripped me up the first time was messing up the cell count. I accidentally hit the shortcut keys too many times or in the wrong order, which left me with extra blank cells and ruined the structure the grader was looking for. I fixed it by clearing everything out, double-checking that I was in command mode by hitting Escape, and doing the sequence one careful step at a time so the final cell layout matched the instructions exactly.


==================================================
TASK 4: Keyboard Shortcuts and Magics
==================================================
Step-by-Step Keyboard Shortcut Demonstration:
1. In command mode (pressed Esc), pressed 'a' to add a new code cell above the initial cell.
2. Typed `x = 10` in this new cell and executed it (Assigned as Execution `In [1]:`).
3. In the cell below, typed `print(x)` and executed it (`In [2]:`).
4. Selected the cell containing `print(x)`, entered command mode (Esc), and pressed 'm' to convert it to a Markdown cell, then typed `# Variable Test` and rendered it.
5. Created a new code cell below, typed `%timeit x + 1`, and executed it (`In [3]:`).

%timeit Output:
27.4 ns ± 0.312 ns per loop (mean ± std. dev. of 7 runs, 10,000,000 loops each)

Explanation of In [n] Counter:
The `In [n]` counters increment based on the order in which code cells are executed by the kernel rather than their physical top-to-bottom layout in the notebook, demonstrating that execution history is tracked independently of cell placement.


